|----------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| Scope and trigger | Replacement of aging or faulty equipment (workstations, printers, switches, UPS) based on asset lifecycle policy, warranty expiry, or failure report. Requirements are based on existing specifications with minor updates for current models. Procurement follows standard purchase order process within approved budget. | Procurement for a new project, office expansion, or technology refresh involving multiple categories of equipment (servers, networking, cabling, software licences). Requirements must be gathered from multiple stakeholders, aligned with project scope and timeline. Procurement follows a formal tender process with evaluation committee approval. |
| Tools and process | Asset register to identify items due for replacement, standard specification template, single or limited quotation from approved vendor panel, purchase order form, delivery and acceptance checklist. Budget approval from department head. | Procurement portal (e.g. e-Perolehan for government-linked organisations), vendor evaluation matrix with weighted scoring, cost comparison spreadsheet, SLA and warranty template, RFQ/ITQ documents, evaluation committee minutes. Budget approval from senior management or project sponsor with justification report. |
| Documentation and reporting | Purchase order, delivery note, acceptance test record, asset tag assignment, and updated asset register. Filed per organisational procurement and audit policy. Simple procurement report summarising items purchased, cost, and vendor. | Complete procurement file including needs analysis, approved specifications, RFQ/ITQ and all vendor responses, evaluation matrix with scoring, committee approval minutes, purchase order, delivery schedule, acceptance test results, warranty certificates, and asset register update. Procurement management report with variance analysis (cost, timeline, specification compliance). |


| Type | Description |
|------|-------------|
| Technical specifications document | A detailed description of the hardware, software, performance, compatibility, warranty, and support requirements for the equipment to be procured. Specifications must be precise enough to ensure that vendor quotations are comparable and that delivered equipment meets organisational needs. The administrator drafts specifications based on user requirements, current technology standards, and compatibility with existing infrastructure. Ambiguous or incomplete specifications are the most common cause of procurement disputes and equipment mismatches. |
| Request for Quotation (RFQ) / Invitation to Quote (ITQ) | A formal document sent to potential vendors inviting them to submit a price quotation for equipment or services matching the technical specifications. The RFQ includes specifications, quantity, delivery requirements, warranty terms, evaluation criteria, and submission deadline. The administrator prepares the RFQ in consultation with the procurement department and ensures it complies with organisational procurement policy and any regulatory requirements (e.g. minimum number of quotations, open tender thresholds). |
| Vendor evaluation matrix (weighted scoring) | A structured tool for comparing vendor quotations against predefined criteria such as price, technical compliance, warranty coverage, delivery timeline, vendor track record, and after-sales support. Each criterion is assigned a weight reflecting its importance to the organisation. Vendors are scored against each criterion and the weighted total determines the recommended vendor. The administrator prepares the matrix, presents it to the evaluation committee, and documents the evaluation rationale for audit and accountability. |
| Service Level Agreement (SLA) and warranty terms | A contractual document that defines the level of service, response times, resolution times, and penalties for non-compliance that the vendor must provide. Warranty terms specify the coverage period, what is covered (parts, labour, on-site service), and the claim process. The administrator reviews SLA and warranty terms during vendor evaluation and negotiates improvements where necessary. Weak SLA or warranty terms can result in extended downtime and additional costs when equipment fails. |
| Asset register and lifecycle management | A centralised database or spreadsheet that records all IT assets including serial number, model, purchase date, warranty expiry, location, assigned user, and disposal date. Lifecycle management tracks each asset from procurement through deployment, maintenance, and eventual disposal or replacement. The administrator updates the asset register at every stage and uses it to plan replacement cycles, track warranty claims, and provide audit evidence. Accurate asset records are essential for budget planning, insurance, and regulatory compliance. |
| Procurement compliance and audit trail | The set of organisational policies, approval workflows, and documentation requirements that govern how procurement is conducted. Compliance ensures that purchases follow approved procedures, budget limits, and vendor selection criteria. The audit trail includes all documents from needs identification to final acceptance, demonstrating that the process was fair, transparent, and properly authorised. The administrator maintains the audit trail and ensures all approvals are obtained before committing expenditure. Non-compliance can result in financial loss, legal liability, or audit findings. |


