| Procurement Document / Tool | Description |
|-----------------------------|-------------|
| Needs analysis report | A document that justifies the procurement by identifying the business need, current equipment deficiencies, user requirements, and expected benefits. Includes a comparison of current vs. required capability, estimated cost, and alignment with organisational IT strategy. The administrator gathers input from users and technical staff, analyses the gap, and presents the report for management approval before proceeding to specification drafting. Without a clear needs analysis, procurement may result in unnecessary or misaligned purchases. |
| Cost comparison spreadsheet | A tabular comparison of all vendor quotations against the same specification items, including unit price, total price, delivery charges, warranty cost, and any optional extras. Normalises pricing to enable like-for-like comparison. The administrator prepares the spreadsheet from received quotations and uses it as supporting evidence for the evaluation matrix. Errors in cost comparison can lead to selection of a more expensive vendor or overlooking hidden costs such as installation, training, or recurring licence fees. |
| Purchase order (PO) | A formal document issued by the organisation to the selected vendor authorising the purchase of specified equipment at the agreed price and terms. The PO includes item descriptions, quantities, unit prices, delivery date, payment terms, and delivery address. The administrator generates the PO after obtaining all required approvals and tracks it through delivery and payment. The PO number is referenced in all subsequent correspondence, delivery notes, and invoices for traceability. |
| Delivery and acceptance checklist | A structured form used during goods receipt to verify that delivered equipment matches the purchase order in terms of quantity, model, serial numbers, and physical condition. Acceptance testing confirms that equipment powers on, passes basic functionality tests, and includes all accessories and documentation (manuals, warranty cards, licence keys). The administrator signs the acceptance form, records serial numbers in the asset register, and files the checklist with the procurement record. Incomplete acceptance checking can result in undetected damage or missing items that are difficult to claim after the delivery window closes. |
| e-Perolehan / procurement portal | An electronic procurement system used by government and government-linked organisations in Malaysia for sourcing, quotation, and purchase order management. Registered vendors submit quotations through the portal, and the system enforces procurement thresholds and approval workflows. The administrator must understand the portal workflow, vendor registration requirements, and how to generate reports for audit. Private-sector organisations may use similar enterprise procurement systems (e.g. SAP Ariba, Oracle Procurement Cloud). |
| Contract and SLA template | A standardised document framework for formalising the agreement between the organisation and the selected vendor. Covers scope of supply, delivery schedule, payment milestones, warranty terms, SLA metrics (response time, resolution time, uptime guarantee), penalty clauses for non-compliance, and dispute resolution. The administrator reviews the template with the procurement or legal department, negotiates terms with the vendor, and ensures both parties sign before delivery commences. A well-drafted contract protects the organisation from vendor underperformance and provides a basis for accountability. |


| Common Issue | Root Cause | Management Action |
|--------------|-----------|-------------------|
| Delivered equipment does not match specifications | Specifications were ambiguous or incomplete; vendor substituted a different model; acceptance checklist not used during delivery | Draft precise specifications with exact model numbers, minimum performance requirements, and compatibility notes; use acceptance checklist to verify every item against the PO before signing; reject non-compliant items immediately |
| Vendor delays delivery beyond agreed timeline | Vendor overcommitted or has supply chain issues; no penalty clause in contract; procurement started too late relative to project deadline | Include delivery timeline and penalty clause in contract/SLA; start procurement early with buffer time; monitor delivery status proactively; escalate to vendor management and consider alternative sourcing if delay is critical |
| Budget overrun on procurement | Initial cost estimate did not include installation, training, cabling, or recurring licence costs; exchange rate fluctuation for imported equipment; scope creep during procurement | Include all total-cost-of-ownership items in the initial budget estimate; lock pricing in the contract; obtain contingency budget approval; use cost comparison spreadsheet to identify and control hidden costs |
| Incomplete procurement documentation for audit | Documents filed inconsistently; approvals obtained verbally but not recorded; asset register not updated after delivery | Establish a procurement filing checklist and enforce it from the start; obtain all approvals in writing or through the procurement portal; update the asset register immediately upon acceptance; conduct periodic audit readiness checks |
| Vendor selected based on lowest price alone, resulting in poor support | Evaluation criteria did not include support quality, warranty terms, or vendor track record; evaluation committee not involved | Use a weighted evaluation matrix that balances price against technical compliance, warranty, support capability, and vendor reputation; involve an evaluation committee for procurement above threshold; document evaluation rationale |


